The current level of functioning of a process should
be judged by its product, not by the quality or
frequency of its public relations press releases.

I'm sure that Enron was very good at producing
glowing press releases.

The DevTeam is very good at producing an excellent,
world renowned, extremely playable, widely lauded
and referenced, low resource demand, multiplatform
computer game.

That they choose to spend their available "NetHack"
time and energy focused strictly on that task,
rather than diffused on conducting public relations,
seems, to me, to be a very wise choice.

You could easily turn a three year update cycle into
a three decade update cycle if every detail had to
be hashed out by them in advance in response to
opinions from everyone in rgrn.

Consider the single issue of "pudding farming", and
how much bandwidth has been spent arguing both sides
of that question here, in the past three years. A
change, if one were appropriate, could be done by
the DevTeam in an hour or less with a snippet of
code, or alternatively using an "open" process with
a few _years_ of fielding arguments about that
change, pro and con, here.

